================================================================================
Generalised oscillator strengths (EELS core-loss cross sections, SpectroEELS)
================================================================================

Sources/SpectroEELS/Resources/GOS/dft-*.json hold the K1, L1 and L23 tables (C, N, O, Mg, Al, Si, Fe, Cu), and
Sources/SpectroEELS/Resources/GOS/dft-gos-tables.bin with dft-gos-index.json the 231 K1, L1, L23, M23 and M45 tables of
95 elements that eXSpy builds its K, L and M edges from (0.12.0), of the GOSH database of Segger, Guzzinati and
Kohl, version 1.5.0, as eXSpy fetches it (Segger_Guzzinati_Kohl_1.5.0.gosh, md5 7fee8891c147a4f769668403b54c529b),
extracted unchanged (every stored float32 value read back bit for bit) by tools/eels-edge/extract_gos.py
(--task16 for the L tables, --all for the pack) into JSON and into raw float32:

    L. Segger, G. Guzzinati, H. Kohl, "A set of Generalised Oscillator Strengths
    for the simulation of EELS spectra", version 1.5.0, Zenodo,
    https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7645765
    Licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International licence
    (CC BY 4.0), https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
    Changes: groups of the HDF5 file re-written as JSON and as raw little-endian float32 with a JSON index;
    no value changed.

The cross section computed from them (Sources/SpectroEELS/EdgeCrossSection.swift)
ports eXSpy's EELSCLEdge and GOS integration (commit 7185a4d1, GPL-3.0-or-later),
SciPy 1.18.1's Simpson rule and interpolating spline (BSD 3-Clause, above), and
the effective collection angle eXSpy translates from Egerton. It is compared with
eXSpy and with pyEELSMODEL (https://github.com/joverbee/pyEELSMODEL, commit
eed9f0721d5b, GPL-3.0) in tools/eels-edge; no Hartree-Slater (Gatan) table is used.


